@c ------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview
@cindex description

@command{psort} reads lines from standard input or one or more files,
counts the occurrences of each unique line, and prints them sorted by
frequency from least to most common.  Each output line shows the
percentage of the total, the raw count, and the value.  A
@samp{Total:} line is printed at the end.

It is the equivalent of:

@example
sort | uniq -c | sort -n
@end example

but with cleaner formatting, aligned columns, percentages, and
additional filtering options.

@example
$ find Movies -type f -name '*.mp4' -o -name '*.m4v' \
    | awk -F. '@{print $NF@}' | psort
  2.8649%    42 mp4
 97.1351%  1424 m4v
   Total:  1466
@end example

@cindex update-alternatives
Four implementations are provided --- Perl, C, Go, and Python 3 --- all
supporting identical command-line options.  When installed via the RPM
or Debian package, @file{/usr/bin/psort} is a symlink managed by
@command{update-alternatives} pointing to @command{psort_c} by default.

@c ------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Options
@chapter Options
@cindex options
@cindex command-line options

@table @option
@item -c
@itemx --csv
@cindex CSV output
Output in CSV format (@samp{percentage%,count,value}) instead of the
human-readable aligned columns.

@item -a
@itemx --csvall
@cindex CSV output, with whitespace replacement
Like @option{--csv}, and also replace all whitespace within each value
with commas.

@item -d @var{N}
@itemx --decimal=@var{N}
@cindex decimal places
Number of decimal places for percentages.  Default is @samp{4}.

@item --numeric
@cindex numeric sort
Sort by the numeric value of the input lines rather than by occurrence
count.  Useful when input lines are numbers and you want them in numeric
order.

@item -m @var{N}
@itemx --min=@var{N}
@cindex minimum count filter
Exclude entries whose count is below @var{N}.  Excluded counts are
accumulated into an @samp{[excluded]} summary line unless
@option{--ignore} is given.  By default, totals and percentages are
recomputed from the remaining entries; see @option{--fullperc} to
override.

@item -x @var{N}
@itemx --max=@var{N}
@cindex maximum count filter
Exclude entries whose count is above @var{N}.  Operates identically to
@option{--min}.

@item -f
@itemx --fullperc
@cindex full percentage
When used with @option{--min} or @option{--max}, compute percentages
from the full original total rather than the filtered count.

@item -i
@itemx --ignore
@cindex suppressing excluded line
When used with @option{--min} or @option{--max}, suppress the
@samp{[excluded]} summary line.

@item -v
@itemx --verbose
@cindex verbose output
When used with @option{--min} or @option{--max}, print to standard
error the number of keys and total count before and after filtering.

@item -r
@itemx --reverse
@cindex reverse sort
Reverse the sort order so the highest count (or highest numeric value
with @option{--numeric}) appears first.

@item -H
@itemx --histogram
@cindex histogram
Display a bar graph alongside each percentage, scaled to the terminal
width.  The bar column precedes the percentage so relative sizes are
immediately visible.  Ignored silently when @option{--csv} or
@option{--csvall} is active.

@item -h
@itemx --help
Print a usage summary and exit.
@end table

