@node Output Format
@chapter Output Format
@cindex output format

@menu
* Default Format::     Normal aligned-column output
* Histogram Format::   Bar-graph output with @option{-H}
* CSV Format::         Machine-readable CSV output
@end menu

@node Default Format
@section Default Format

The default (human-readable) output format per entry is:

@example
@var{percentage}%  @var{count} @var{value}
@end example

Percentages below 10 are padded with a leading space so decimal points
align.  The count column is right-justified to the width of the largest
count.

@node Histogram Format
@section Histogram Format

With @option{-H} / @option{--histogram}, each entry is prefixed by a
bar proportional to its percentage:

@example
[@var{bar}] @var{percentage}%  @var{count} @var{value}
@end example

The bar width is computed from the terminal width (via @code{TIOCGWINSZ}
or @code{shutil.get_terminal_size}) minus the space needed for the
fixed-width columns, so the bars fill most of the available line.  If
the terminal width cannot be determined, 80 columns is assumed.  The
@env{COLUMNS} environment variable overrides the detected width.

The @samp{Total:} line is indented to keep its count field aligned with
the count column above it.

@option{--histogram} is silently ignored when @option{--csv} or
@option{--csvall} is active.

@node CSV Format
@section CSV Format

CSV output (@option{-c} or @option{-a}):

@example
@var{percentage}%,@var{count},@var{value}
@end example

The final line in either format is:

@example
  Total:  @var{N}
@end example

When @option{--min} or @option{--max} filtering is active and excluded
entries exist, an additional @samp{[excluded]} line appears before the
total (unless @option{--ignore} suppresses it).

@c ------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Examples
@chapter Examples
@cindex examples

Count file extensions under the current directory, least common first:

@example
find . -type f | sed 's/.*\.//' | psort
@end example

Show the five most common HTTP status codes (highest count first):

@example
awk '@{print $9@}' access.log | psort -r | head -5
@end example

Word frequencies, keeping only words appearing at least 10 times,
percentages computed from the full document total:

@example
tr -cs 'A-Za-z' '\n' < doc.txt | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z' | psort --min 10 --fullperc
@end example

Numeric distribution of response times, sorted by value:

@example
awk '@{print $NF@}' timings.log | psort --numeric
@end example

CSV of entries with counts between 5 and 100, full-total percentages:

@example
cat data.txt | psort -c --min 5 --max 100 --fullperc
@end example

@c ------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Implementations
@chapter Implementations
@cindex implementations
@cindex ports

Four implementations are provided, all supporting identical
command-line options:

@table @command
@item psort.pl
@cindex Perl implementation
The original implementation, written in Perl.  Uses a hash
(@code{%data}) for counting and Perl's built-in sort for ordering.
Requires Perl and the @samp{Getopt::Long} module (included in standard
Perl distributions).

@item psort_c
@cindex C implementation
@cindex hash table, open-addressing
C implementation.  Uses an open-addressing hash table with FNV-1a
hashing and dynamic resizing at a 65% load factor.  No external
dependencies; links only against libc.

@item psort_go
@cindex Go implementation
Go implementation.  Uses Go's built-in @code{map[string]int} for
counting and @code{sort.Slice} for ordering.  Compiled to a
self-contained static binary with no runtime dependencies.

@item psort.py
@cindex Python implementation
Python 3 implementation.  Uses a @code{dict} for counting and
Python's built-in @code{sorted()} for ordering.  Requires Python 3
(standard library only; no third-party packages).
@end table

@cindex update-alternatives
When installed from a package, @file{/usr/bin/psort} is a symlink
managed by @command{update-alternatives}.  Installed priorities:

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .30 .40
@headitem Binary @tab Priority @tab Notes
@item @command{psort_c}  @tab 40 @tab Default
@item @command{psort_go} @tab 30 @tab
@item @command{psort.py} @tab 20 @tab
@item @command{psort.pl} @tab 10 @tab
@end multitable

To switch implementations:

@example
update-alternatives --config psort
@end example

