@node Scaling
@section Scaling Behavior
@cindex scaling
@cindex input size

A scaling benchmark was run at input sizes from 100,000 to 650,000
lines in 50,000-line increments, with 3 timed runs per variant per size
point.  The results were plotted as time-vs-size and memory-vs-size
charts (@file{scaling_real.png}, @file{scaling_user.png},
@file{scaling_sys.png}, @file{scaling_mem.png} in the source tree).

All four ports show broadly linear real-time growth once the correct
hash-table implementation is in place.  @command{psort_c} is fastest at
every size point; @command{psort_go} is second; @command{psort.py} and
@command{psort.pl} follow, with Perl's larger per-key hash overhead
becoming more apparent at higher line counts.

Memory consumption scales proportionally to the number of @emph{unique}
lines encountered.  On a dataset of file paths (high uniqueness) it
grows roughly linearly with input size across all ports.

Before the hash table fix in @command{psort_c} (@pxref{The C Hash
Bug}), the scaling curve was visibly quadratic and the 650,000-line run
timed out entirely.

@node The C Hash Bug
@section The O(n^2) Bug in psort_c
@cindex O(n^2) bug
@cindex hash table
@cindex FNV-1a

The original C port used a flat array for counting: every new input
line triggered a linear scan through all previously-seen keys to find a
match.  This is O(n) per line and O(n^2) overall.

@subsection Symptoms

On a 107,000-line benchmark the quadratic cost was small enough (under
0.2 s) to go unnoticed.  On the 100,000--650,000-line scaling
benchmark, the growth curve was unmistakably quadratic and the 650,000-line
run timed out.  On the full 2,289,195-line input the process ran for
over four minutes before being killed.

@subsection Fix

The fix replaced the linear scan with an open-addressing hash table:

@table @strong
@item Hash function
FNV-1a (Fowler-Noll-Vo 1a) --- a simple, non-cryptographic hash
function with good distribution for short string keys.

@item Collision resolution
Linear probing.  Slots are probed sequentially until an empty slot or
matching key is found.

@item Load factor
The table is doubled when 65% of slots are occupied, keeping probe
sequences short and lookup O(1) amortized.

@item Key storage
Keys are heap-allocated via @code{strdup}; the hash table stores
pointers and avoids copying strings on every lookup.
@end table

After the fix, @command{psort_c} went from last place (unusable at
scale) to first place on every metric: wall time, user CPU, sys CPU,
and peak memory.

@node Observations
@section Observations
@cindex observations

@subheading C is fastest on every metric

With the hash table corrected, @command{psort_c} leads on real time
(0.519 s average), user CPU (0.460 s), sys CPU (0.054 s), and peak
memory (67.33 MB).  No interpreter, no garbage collector, no runtime
overhead --- just a tight read loop and one hash lookup per line.

@subheading Go is a close second, but GC shows in sys time

Go's 0.949 s average real time is 83% slower than C but well ahead of
the interpreted ports.  The standout cost is sys CPU (0.182 s average
--- three times more than C or Python), a direct consequence of Go's
concurrent garbage collector making write-barrier and heap-scan passes
that require kernel interaction.  For a workload this memory-intensive
(2.3 million unique strings), GC pressure is the dominant cost above
raw computation.

@subheading Python beats Perl at scale and nearly matches Go

Python (1.055 s) is only 11% behind Go and 25% faster than Perl
(1.402 s).  CPython's dict scales efficiently to large key counts, and
its sys CPU (0.056 s) is essentially identical to C --- no GC churn.
Python also uses the second-least memory (84.55 MB) --- less than Go's
runtime overhead (100.27 MB).

@subheading Perl holds last place at large scale

Perl was competitive on the original 107,000-line benchmark but does
not scale as well to 2.3 million lines.  At 1.402 s it is 2.7x slower
than C and 33% slower than Python.  Perl's hash carries more per-key
overhead than Python's dict (135 MB vs. 85 MB), which compounds at
large unique key counts.

